  <ctml:notes>The fifth World Chess Championship match, 15 March-26 May 1894. Emanuel Lasker defeated defending champion Wilhelm (William) Steinitz, becoming the second world champion. Match condition: first to win ten games; draws did not count toward the winning target. Lasker +10-5=4; points 12.0-7.0 over 19 games. The supplied narrative reports stakes of $2,000 per side and a time control of 15 moves per hour. Its historical commentary and quotations are preserved verbatim in the source record below. Corrections: Lasker's five consecutive wins were games 7-11, spanning New York (7-8) and Philadelphia (9-11), not all Philadelphia. After game 14 the win count was 7-4 and the point score 8.5-5.5. The match ended when Lasker achieved his tenth win in game 19. The PGN supplies all dates, colors, results and main-line moves. PGN ECO classifications take precedence in the eco elements; differing pasted classifications remain in each game's source note. Opening names come from the pasted list. The unexplained +94/-94 table values and PGN Elo 2714/2631 are preserved without assigning a rating system. The multi-country venue is recorded as text: the schema permits only one header placeRef/federation. Per-game dates and city assignments are in source notes because CTML 2.1 GameType has no date/place fields. Nineteen games were played; header rounds is omitted because its schema meaning is scheduled rounds, whereas this match was first-to-ten-wins. No new ratings, FIDE identifiers, clock readings or unrecorded resignation reasons are inferred.</ctml:notes>
